---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { specificationDoc } from '../../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="Context permissions — public and private depth in one pod"
  description="Contexts are the current sempods authorization boundary. Grants are resolved server-side, so resources can hold public and private statements at the same URI."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">concepts · permissions</p>
  <h1>Same resource, different depth</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An event has one address. Its date can be public while its booking notes
    stay private. Contexts let the current sempods contract express that
    difference without creating a second event.
  </p>
  <pre class="ascii-diagram" role="img" data-no-copy aria-label="One event has public facts and private notes in separate Contexts">{`same event URI
      |
      +--> public Context
      |      title, date, location
      |
      +--> private Context
             notes, drafts, planning`}</pre>
  <h2>A Context is a permission boundary</h2>
  <p>
    A Context is an RDF named graph. Every statement belongs to exactly one.
    Grants name its IRI with <code>#read</code>, <code>#write</code> or{' '}<code>#manage</code>. An app can receive access to public events and private
    planning without receiving access to contacts.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pod resolves grants on each request. They are durable server-side
    policy, separate from the coarse OAuth scopes carried in a token.
    Revoking a grant changes what the next request may reach.
  </p>
  <h2>One decision across the data surfaces</h2>
  <p>
    Resource reads, <code>find</code>, SPARQL and optional MCP tools use the
    caller's readable Contexts. A query cannot gain access by naming a private
    graph. A write needs authority over its explicit target Context.
  </p>
  <p>
    Clients can narrow a read to selected Contexts. Unknown or unreadable
    selections add no data; the result does not disclose the private catalogue.
  </p>
  <h2>Lifecycle is optional; the current boundary is core</h2>
  <p>
    Every current core implementation supports the Context contract. Creating
    and deleting Contexts through HTTP is a separate optional module. A
    deployment can provision its Contexts another way.
  </p>
  <h2>The direction is broader</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={specificationDoc('docs/vision.md')}>vision</a> leaves room for
    different storage and policy models. <a href={specificationDoc('docs/proposals/data-access.md')}>Authorized data access</a>{' '}proposes ordinary operations without Context setup, while retaining discovery
    and selection of named views. That proposal has not replaced the current contract.
  </p>
  <p>
    The aim is a small common interface: the pod protects the data, and clients
    can use it without adopting its internal policy representation.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href={specificationDoc('spec/core/contexts.md')}>Context contract</a>{' '}
    <a href={specificationDoc('spec/core/grants.md')}>Grant contract</a>{' '}
    <a href={specificationDoc('spec/modules/context-management.md')}>Optional lifecycle module</a>{' '}
    <a href="/concepts/graph-retrieval">Explore retrieval</a>
  </p>
</Base>
